From 0df8e82b3b958cadcea1c3e2a18667030ba88314 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 20:46:15 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] rocket RK3576: fill the first conv's unreached weight lanes
 with the neutral value

Each 16 byte unit of the ARGB layout is three [R,G,B,pad] groups and a fourth
group that no kx reaches, because kx runs 0 to 2. Those four bytes kept
whatever the memset left, which was zero.

A stored byte is w - 0x80 and the hardware subtracts the weight zero point
through B, so a lane's effective weight is stored - (wt_zp - 0x80). Zero is
therefore not nothing: for conv0, whose weight zero point is 151, it is -23.
Twelve lanes per output channel, three ky by four bytes, multiply real input
data by -23.

The neutral value is pad, which is exactly what the alpha lane beside them
already gets. This is the same mistake the depthwise weight buffer had in its
zero point lanes, and it hides on any per-axis tensor because there the zero
point is 0 and the two agree; conv0 is the only per-tensor weight tensor in
this project whose zero point is not 128.

Found by exclusion: the multiplier's magnitude was swept and moved the fitted a
exactly as computed while leaving the residual identical at every scale, so
what remained was a per-channel multiplicative error, which a global multiplier
cannot produce.

ROCKET_FC_ZERO_PAD restores the zero fill as a control.

Signed-off-by: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
---
 src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c | 21 ++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c b/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c
index cdd2444..265b0b0 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c
@@ -297,7 +297,26 @@ rkt_fill_weights(struct rkt_ml_subgraph *subgraph,
       uint8_t pad = zero_point - 0x80;
       unsigned oc_n = output_channels_real;
 
-      memset(weights_out, 0, weights_size);
+      /*
+       * Fill with the NEUTRAL weight, not with zero.
+       *
+       * Each 16 byte unit holds three [R,G,B,pad] groups and a fourth group
+       * that no kx reaches, so those four bytes keep whatever the memset left.
+       * A stored byte is w - 0x80 and the hardware subtracts the weight zero
+       * point through B, so the effective weight of a lane is
+       * stored - (wt_zp - 0x80). Zero therefore means -(wt_zp - 0x80), which
+       * is -23 for conv0 whose weight zero point is 151, not nothing: twelve
+       * lanes per output channel multiply real input data by -23.
+       *
+       * The neutral value is pad, which is exactly what the alpha lane beside
+       * them already gets. This is the same mistake the depthwise buffer had
+       * in its zero point lanes, and it hides on any per-axis tensor because
+       * there the zero point is 0 and the two agree.
+       *
+       * ROCKET_FC_ZERO_PAD restores the memset to zero as the control.
+       */
+      memset(weights_out, getenv("ROCKET_FC_ZERO_PAD") ? 0 : pad,
+             weights_size);
 
       for (int ky = 0; ky < weights_width; ky++) {
          for (unsigned oc = 0; oc < oc_n; oc++) {
-- 
